snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/2/code.py:35:#      inside the blocked tail and keeps the obstacle effect readable.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/2/code.py:41:#      That guarantees a visible blank tail behind the magenta obstacle, so the variant does not collapse back to the original task.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/2/code.py:279:        # and at least one blank tail cell behind it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b5ca7ac4/D8/2/code.py:22:#      copy whose border pixels are recolored to red while keeping the 3x3 interior unchanged.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b5ca7ac4/D8/2/code.py:26:#      for the solver to infer that movement and recoloring are coupled.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b5ca7ac4/D8/2/code.py:64:    \"\"\"Return a copy whose border pixels are recolored and interior is preserved.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b5ca7ac4/D8/2/code.py:89:    stacking logic as before, but paste a border-recolored copy of each square into the output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D4/1/code.py:16:#    Objects stay the same as the original task: 3-cell L-shaped arrows plus 1-cell or 3-cell walls.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D4/1/code.py:18:#    the bounce cell was recolored grey and all later ray cells also became grey. In this variant, that same\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D4/1/code.py:21:#    Example: arrow color 2, vertical wall color 5 adjacent to bounce cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D4/1/code.py:26:#    If we allowed all arrows to have zero bounces, the sample could look identical to the original task and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D4/1/code.py:28:#    the invariant-color rule visually obvious, wall colors are sampled from colors not used by arrows, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D4/1/code.py:29:#    human sees repeated cases where a differently colored wall reflects the ray without recoloring it.\n/home/