/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:8:#   arrows:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:14:#       colors: exactly two colors with multiplicities 8 + 1\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:23:#     ray: diagonal trace, side-touch reflection, color inheritance, stop at exit/other arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:34:#     Input arrow block:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:44:#   the arrow is no longer an L, so the solver must use the odd color, not the silhouette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:45:#   Each generated grid uses at least two arrows and enforces at least two distinct directions to make\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:49:#   - Arrow detection by per-color 4-connected components only works for monochrome arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:51:#   - start_col == arrow color assumed every arrow pixel shared one color; now only the body color should seed the ray.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:52:#   - wall_cells = non-bg minus arrow_cells is still valid, but only after arrow detection is upgraded.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:56:#   - each arrow is an isolated 2x2 non-bg component with no orthogonal non-bg neighbors outside the block;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:57:#   - no overlap between arrows, walls, and planned ray cells;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:58:#   - walls stay out of the arrow's 4-neighborhood so arrow recognition is stable;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:64:#   Because multiple arrows with different marker corners appear together, a human can line up marker positions\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/142ca369/D18/1/code.py:68:#   Arrow blocks are isolated from other input objects in 4-neighborhood terms, so no wall touches an arrow 