ocating every legend first, filtering counts by the visible key colors only, and then rendering all answers with the same dot color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D7/1/code.py:18:#    - The template is still cropped and recolored, but the stencil no longer contributes\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D7/1/code.py:30:#      The changed step is exactly the \"expand repetitions\" stage; the split/crop/recolor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D7/1/code.py:151:    - Crop section 1 to its non-black bbox, recolor it with the two swatches, and interpret\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D2/2/code.py:31:#     the origin copy is recolored to O\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D2/2/code.py:53:#   multiple examples per grid instead of a one-off hidden parameter\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D2/2/code.py:57:#     they assume every non-zero delta is an integer multiple of one global\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D2/2/code.py:131:    - The origin copy is recolored to O, and any overlapped pixels are also recolored to O.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/3/code.py:21:#   flipped left-right before recoloring. Example:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/3/code.py:25:#   The original task would recolor the left matrix directly; this variant recolors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/3/code.py:39:#     insert `np.fliplr(...)` before recoloring.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/3/code.py:88:    Mirror a cropped template horizontally, recolor it, and repeat it from a count stencil.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/3/code.py:96:    - Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels, flip that crop left-right, then recolor it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/3/code.py:98:    - Tile the mirrored recolored crop along the separator axis, inserting one background-colored row or column between copies.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D7/2/code.py:9:#      color. The output contains one recolored copy per blob, and that copy's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D7/2/code.py:48:#      No region mixes multiple semantics on top of each other.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D7/2/code.py:133:    - Read the 4-connected blobs from section 2 in layout order, and for each blob recolor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58490d8a/27/code.py:35:#    - Body colors are sampled from a tiny shared pool, often repeated across multiple connector\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58490d8a/27/code.py:40:#    - Generation forces at least one keyed cluster to contain multiple distinct body shapes and at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58490d8a/27/code.py:80:#    - At least one keyed cluster exhibits both fusion effects: multiple body shapes and a nontrivial\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:9:#    - 本变体把“空框”改成“提示框”：它的内部用保留色 7 画一支 8 向箭头，箭头表示训练对的有向关系，\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:11:#    - 具体小例子：若两个同配色训练框分别位于左上和右下，提示框中的箭头若指向右下，\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:12:#      则 solver 必须把左上当源、右下当目标；若箭头反向，则应交换两者。原始 solver 会双向尝试再打分，\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:13:#      变体 solver 则先读箭头，再只按箭头指定方向推断模板并作用到测试框。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:17:#      （如镜像、自反反色），即使有箭头也不会改变测试输出，会退化回原题。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:25:#    - 提示箭头只画在提示框内部，并且保留色 7 不出现在训练/测试精灵、边框和背景中，避免识别冲突。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:27:#    - 人类看到样例时，可以先注意到唯一的保留色箭头，再观察它指向哪一个训练框。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:30:#    - 箭头只占提示框内部，不叠加到其他对象上；背景和边框颜色与提示色、精灵色相互避让。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:375:def arrow_mask(n: int, direction: tuple[int, int]) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:380:    tail_r = 0 if dr > 0 else (n - 1 if dr < 0 else mid)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:381:    tail_c = 0 if dc > 0 else (n - 1 if dc < 0 else mid)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:382:    tip_r = n - 1 if dr > 0 else (0 if dr < 0 else mid)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:383:    tip_c = n - 1 if dc > 0 else (0 if dc < 0 else mid)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:385:    draw_line(mask, tail_r, tail_c, end_r=tip_r, end_c=tip_c, color=1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:389:    base_r = tip_r - dr\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:390:    base_c = tip_c - dc\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:394:    mask[tip_r, tip_c] = 1\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:399:    return arrow_mask(n, direction) * HINT_COLOR\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:407:        if np.array_equal(mask, arrow_mask(inner.shape[0], direction)):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:447:    - The remaining frame is no longer empty: it contains a color-7 arrow that points from the training source frame toward the training destination frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:450:    - Detect the hint frame by its reserved arrow color, decode the arrow direction, and use that direction to order the two matched training frames.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D19/2/code.py:455:    - The extra frame is now active metadata: it tells you how to read the analogy, not what to draw directly. The puzzle is “arrow says A -> B, so apply the same rule to C”.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8b9c3697/D1/0/code.py:12:#    - Each movable block is still mostly red, but its contact face is recolored to the matching tooth color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/0/code.py:19:#     original: the origin-guided copy was recolored entirely to O.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/0/code.py:33:#       - recolor every pixel of the origin copy to O\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/0/code.py:38:#       - recolor only the origin copy's border support to O\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/0/code.py:54:#       origin-covered pixel needing recolor. That assumption is no longer valid; we now need a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/0/code.py:55:#       finer `origin_border` mask instead of recoloring the full origin support.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/0/code.py:56:#     - Overlap recoloring remains safe as a final overwrite because O is still the intended color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/0/code.py:148:      recolored to O, while its interior pixels remain T.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/0/code.py:149:    - Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/0/code.py:219:MIN_INTERIOR_CELLS = 1             # ensure the border-only recolor is visible\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/2/code.py:18:#   black with a 1-cell black border. The output still repeats one recolored tile N times,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/2/code.py:20:#   is that the cropped template mask is rotated 90 degrees clockwise before recoloring.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/2/code.py:22:#   Original: a cropped 2x3 mask shaped like [[1,1,0],[0,1,1]] becomes a 2x3 recolored\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/2/code.py:25:#   foreground/background cells are recolored and tiled. The repeat count and separator\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/2/code.py:29:#   source idea of a visible rotation without adding multiple branches such as mirror vs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/2/code.py:83:    Rotate a cropped template, recolor it, and repeat it from a component-count stencil.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/2/code.py:91:    - Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels, rotate that crop 90 degrees clockwise, and recolor it with the two swatches.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D2/2/code.py:93:    - Repeat the rotated recolored tile that many times, inserting one background-colored separator row/column between copies.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:3:# Name: 箭头标记反射方向 + 反射轴由锚块特征决定\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:26:#   1. one green arrow glyph placed near the target anchor,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:28:# - The arrow gives the mandatory \"primary\" reflection:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:29:#     right arrow  -> LR reflection is required,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:30:#     down arrow   -> UD reflection is required.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:32:#   also needed. Concretely, after reading the arrow, the solver compares the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:38:#     1. read the local arrow; if it is a down arrow, start from UD instead,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:42:#   Difference: quadrant position no longer selects the mirror; the arrow gives\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:46:# - The arrow vocabulary stays tiny: exactly two 3x3 glyphs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:49:#   arrows alone are insufficient, and anchors alone are ambiguous without first\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:50:#   knowing which primary axis the arrow selected.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:60:#   must remove arrow markers before stamping or they would leak into the output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:62:#   arrows exist only outside TL, and the TL motif remains one 4-connected\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:66:# - The right arrow is drawn 1 cell above the anchor; the down arrow is drawn 1\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:71:# - The arrow is kept disconnected from the anchor, so component extraction for\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:75:# - A human can read the arrow as the obvious first instruction, then notice\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:76:#   that two targets with the same arrow can still differ because of the anchor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:78:# - The generator enforces both arrow types, both anchor states, and at least\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:82:# - Green is reserved for arrows and never used inside the motif.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:84:#   anchor, while arrows sit outside with a one-cell gap. This avoids overlap and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:88:# - No hidden color ordering is used. Red still marks anchors, green means arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:91:#   \"arrows only\" or \"original quadrant rule\" at the whole-output level.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:95:# - Every non-TL quadrant has exactly one arrow glyph and exactly one decorated\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:97:# - Each target anchor matches exactly one of two templates once its arrow-chosen\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:212:def _read_arrow_primaries(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:225:        assert key != (0, 0), \"arrows are only allowed in non-TL quadrants\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:226:        assert key not in primaries, \"each non-TL quadrant must contain exactly one arrow glyph\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:229:            raise AssertionError(\"unrecognized arrow glyph\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:233:        assert key in primaries, \"each non-TL quadrant must contain exactly one arrow glyph\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:254:    Replicate the TL motif across quadrants with size scaling, arrow-selected primary reflection, and anchor-selected extra reflection.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:259:    - Each non-TL quadrant contains one decorated red anchor plus exactly one nearby green arrow glyph; the anchor size still varies by quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:263:    - In each target quadrant, read the arrow first to get the mandatory reflection axis: right arrow means LR, down arrow means UD.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:264:    - Compare the observed target-anchor template against the TL anchor under either that primary flip alone or both flips; apply the matching transform to the full source motif, scale by anchor-size ratio, align the red region to the target anchor, and stamp non-background pixels. Remove the green arrows from the final output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:266:    Key insight: The arrow does not fully specify the transform by itself. It only chooses the primary axis; the anchor's internal asymmetry tells you whether an additional orthogonal flip must be composed on top of it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:275:    primaries, marker_components = _read_arrow_primaries(g, mid_r, mid_c)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:515:def _paint_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, top: int, left: int, shape: frozenset[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:520:def _place_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, anchor_r: int, anchor_c: int, primary: tuple[bool, bool]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:523:        _paint_arrow(grid, anchor_r - 4, anchor_c, shape)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:525:        _paint_arrow(grid, anchor_r, anchor_c - 4, shape)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:611:    arrow_only_out = _build_output(patch, anchor_patch, tl_r0, tl_c0, target_sizes, anchors, primaries)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:613:    if int(np.count_nonzero(out != arrow_only_out)) < MIN_VARIANT_DIFF:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:624:        _place_arrow(inp, ar, ac, primaries[key])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:627:    # it is the reason arrow_only_out differs from out.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58490d8a/8/code.py:49:#    - Reused body colors across multiple clusters make the connector-based match visible.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3e6067c3/D1/2/code.py:56:#    - Assumption: when multiple frames share a size, the intended next frame is the best\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/1/code.py:54:#   detail; in the variant it matters because the origin writes background over\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/1/code.py:59:# - Because translations are integer multiples of (h, w), distinct copies have\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D8/1/code.py:105:    The special T-colored singleton still marks the reference copy, but now that copy is rendered as the bbox-level inverse of the template rather than as a recolored duplicate.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8b9c3697/D1/3/code.py:14:#    - The comb/bracket structures stay unchanged: a single opening side leads into a narrow cavity.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D8/2/code.py:19:#    template pixels were cropped to their bbox and recolored directly. Now the cropped yellow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D8/2/code.py:20:#    silhouette is first completed to its convex hull, then recolored. Example: a 2x2 L-shape\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D8/2/code.py:21:#    template `[[4,4],[4,0]]` used to stay an L after recoloring; in this variant it becomes a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D8/2/code.py:27:#    the original crop-and-recolor task. To avoid that, generation rejects shapes whose convex-hull\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D8/2/code.py:46:#    recoloring. This is visible in pixel geometry alone; no ordering by color id or hidden numeric\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D8/2/code.py:159:    Complete a cropped template to its convex hull, recolor it, and repeat it by stencil count.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D8/2/code.py:168:      silhouette before recoloring it with the two swatch colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D8/2/code.py:170:    - Repeat the completed/recolored tile that many times, separating copies by one row or column\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D8/2/code.py:174:    completion of the cropped template into its convex hull before any recoloring or tiling.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3e6067c3/D1/4/code.py:14:#   D1 change visible in the output instead of remaining a hidden lookup detail.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D15/0/code.py:56:- Colors are reused across multiple objects when possible so the legend is not a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D15/0/code.py:112:- There is no recoloring transformation. Colors act only as category membership\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/269e22fb/D5/0/code.py:47:#       miss part of the 25-label seed support, leaving multiple legal completions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D1/1/code.py:21:#      is recolored to O in the output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D1/1/code.py:29:#    and overlap recoloring stay the same. To keep the visual cue strong, the generator enforces a unique\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D1/1/code.py:66:    - Singleton pixels elsewhere all share one color O; none is specially recolored.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D1/1/code.py:72:    - Stamp the template at those offsets, scaled by template height/width; the origin's copy is recolored to O, and multi-covered pixels are also recolored to O.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D1/1/code.py:90:def _recolor_cropped_template(mask: np.ndarray, edge_color: int, interior_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D1/1/code.py:109:    - Inside that crop, leave black cells black; recolor yellow cells that touch black in the 4-neighborhood as the section-3 color; recolor the remaining yellow cells as the section-4 color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D1/1/code.py:110:    - Count the 4-connected components in section 2 and repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one black separator row or column between copies.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D1/1/code.py:126:    tile = _recolor_cropped_template(cropped_mask, edge_color, interior_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D1/1/code.py:264:    tile = _recolor_cropped_template(shape_mask, edge_color, interior_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3e6067c3/D13/0/code.py:43:#    A scene is accepted only if at least one cell is painted by multiple bridges and the D13 output\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D1/4/code.py:124:    - Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D2/3/code.py:20:#   color matcher would see multiple possible pairs, while the new logic picks the two size-6\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58490d8a/1/code.py:63:#      cells plus a right tail/slack. This replaces the old spaced-dot width.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D20/0/code.py:41:#    tricks. Candidate multiplicity is kept small (usually 3-10 templates), and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D20/0/code.py:699:def recolor_sprite(arr: np.ndarray, src_colors: list[int], dst_colors: list[int]) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D20/0/code.py:827:    train_src = recolor_sprite(_as_array(case[\"train_src\"]), [1, 2], p1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D20/0/code.py:828:    train_dst = recolor_sprite(_as_array(case[\"train_dst\"]), [1, 2], p1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D20/0/code.py:829:    test_src = recolor_sprite(_as_array(case[\"test_src\"]), [3, 4], p2)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D20/0/code.py:830:    test_dst = recolor_sprite(_as_array(case[\"test_dst\"]), [3, 4], p2)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/269e22fb/D9/0/code.py:68:#     - `len(colors) == 2` still holds because the motif is still recolored as foreground vs\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D3/0/code.py:28:    origin copy recolors every template cell to O\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D3/0/code.py:29:    any accidental overlap is also recolored to O\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D3/0/code.py:36:- Original task behavior would stamp three monochrome copies and recolor the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D3/0/code.py:67:  separate recolor signal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D3/0/code.py:86:  pile of conflicting colors; only the origin copy is uniformly recolored.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D3/0/code.py:89:- There is only one recolor rule: every cell of the origin copy becomes O.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D3/0/code.py:127:    - Non-origin copies keep the template's per-cell colors. The origin copy recolors every stamped cell to O.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D3/0/code.py:128:    - If two copies ever cover the same output cell, that cell is also recolored to O.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D3/0/code.py:132:    template palette identifies the one copy