eshi/code/3e6067c3/D12/0/code.py:211:    - When the next control color appears on multiple bridgeable squares, paint bridges from the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D8/3/code.py:24:#    - This keeps the change obvious to humans without multiplying the visual\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D3/1/code.py:87:    - Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels and recolor it with the two swatches.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b0039139/D3/1/code.py:89:    - Repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one background-colored row/column between copies.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/_parent_retest_20260516.json:241:    \"name\": \"箭头标记反射方向 + 反射轴由锚块特征决定\",\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/_parent_retest_20260516.json:335:    \"name\": \"显式分割线 + 连接器匹配 + 箭头标记反射\",\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3e6067c3/D10/2/code.py:10:#      When a control color matches multiple candidate squares, the solver must still prefer row/column-band aligned\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58490d8a/7/code.py:66:#    - No recoloring is introduced. A row's color always matches one input key color and one outside sprite set.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:71:     rejecting any sample where the D7 recolor would not visibly differ from the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:72:     original non-recolor behavior.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:79:     unchanged after recoloring.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:86:   - Because multiple differently colored frames appear in the same input distribution,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:87:     the solver can infer a type-conditioned recolor rather than a global palette swap.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:92:   - The solver assumes the frame shape remains a standard 4x4 ring after recoloring.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:135:    recolor_frames: bool,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:141:        patch = _canonical_frame(bg) if recolor_frames else sub\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:156:    - Every frame is recolored to one fixed output color before stacking.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:162:    return _build_output(frames, solids, bg, recolor_frames=True)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:209:        out_new = _build_output(frames, solids, bg, recolor_frames=True)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2ba387bc/D7/0/code.py:210:        out_old = _build_output(frames, solids, bg, recolor_frames=False)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3e6067c3/D6/1/code.py:9:#    participates in the traversed control path becomes \"visited\": its outer frame is recolored\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3e6067c3/D6/1/code.py:17:#    If every square in every generated scene were on the path, \"visited squares recolor\" would\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3e6067c3/D6/1/code.py:24:#      remains true because recoloring happens only in the output, not the input.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3e6067c3/D6/1/code.py:47:#    The recolor rule is structural, not palette-dependent: the border becomes the same color as\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3e6067c3/D6/1/code.py:165:    - Every square that is actually visited by the traversed path is recolored into a solid block\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D12/0/code.py:62:    - Recolor the origin copy to O, and recolor any cell covered by two or more stamped copies to O as well.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D11/0/code.py:9:#     - The D11 change is a single-color thin arrow placed in the open corridor between those two\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c7dc4dd/D11/0/code.py:10:#       training frames. The arrow is horizontal or vertical and points from source frame to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/c