cabulary is intentionally small: only two 3x3 arrow glyphs exist,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:48:#   anchors. With markers added, leaving `out` unchanged would leak arrows into the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:55:# - Marker arrows are placed relative to each target anchor with a one-cell gap:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:56:#   the LR arrow sits above the anchor, the UD arrow sits left of the anchor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:80:# - Each non-TL quadrant has one or two green arrow components and at least one arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:82:# - Each arrow glyph matches one of the two exact templates below.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:203:        assert grouped[key] >= 1, \"each non-TL quadrant must contain at least one arrow marker\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:210:    Copy the TL motif into the other quadrants, scaling by anchor size and mirroring by arrow markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:215:    - Each non-TL quadrant additionally contains one or two green arrow glyphs: a right arrow requests LR reflection, and a down arrow requests UD reflection.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:219:    - For each target quadrant, read its arrow glyphs to decide which reflections to apply, then scale the transformed motif by the ratio between target-anchor size and TL-anchor size.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:222:    Key insight: The red anchor still tells you the scale, but mirror behavior is no longer implied by quadrant position; it is written explicitly by the nearby arrow symbols.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:443:def _paint_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, top: int, left: int, shape: frozenset[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:450:        _paint_arrow(grid, anchor_r - 4, anchor_c, RIGHT_ARROW)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:452:        _paint_arrow(grid, anchor_r, anchor_c - 4, DOWN_ARROW)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/3/code.py:55:    - the source motif uses multiple colors and an asymmetric layout, making 90/180/270 degree outputs distinguishable\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/3/code.py:206:    - Each non-TL anchor has a short non-red marker ray attached to one side; its length is 1, 2, or 3 pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/53fb4810/D19/1/code.py:282:    - Each plus has one true seed strip touching exactly one arm tip; some long rays in the input\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/53fb4810/D19/1/code.py:292:    the canonical seed is the fixed two-cell-deep strip immediately outside the matching arm tip.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/35ab12c3/D10/1/code.py:19:#   The change is narrow: distractors, colors, shadow mechanics, and spacing remain the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7c66cb00/3/code.py:43:# - The destination structure changes visibly: wide bottom bars become narrow side bars.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D5/1/code.py:189:    - Detect which bbox diagonal carries the L-shaped anchors; the other diagonal defines the target diamond exactly as in the base task.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7c66cb00/11/code.py:28:#      creating multiple descendants across bars.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D5/3/code.py:47:#     - Overlapping bboxes are disallowed in generation to avoid clutter from multiple swaps in the same area.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D5/3/code.py:131:    - Away from the legend, each marker color appears either as an L-shaped diagonal corner pair or as a solid 2x2 block.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D4/3/code.py:53:#     - The L-shaped 2x2 logical motif guarantees visible holes/gaps, so the result is not mistaken for\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/53fb4810/D2/0/code.py:22:#    - Direction only has four cardinal values, and the asymmetric cue is repeated across multiple objects per grid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/53fb4810/D2/0/code.py:28:#    - Original alignment asserts matched the seed bbox to the emitting arm tip. Those asserts are also invalid here,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7c66cb00/7/code.py:274:    - Detect each whole top sprite as one connected object, even if it has multiple colors and bg holes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D3/0/code.py:45:#     - Dot spacing stays uniform, so human solvers can compare wide vs narrow frames and infer capacity from shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7c66cb00/17/code.py:43:#   - At least one sprite gets an inner region when possible, so the legend acts on multiple\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:3:# Name: 箭头头部对准目标点\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:20:# - The head is no longer a full edge line. It is a fixed 3x3 arrow sprite:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:26:#   in the arrow's pointing direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:27:# - The translation distance is measured from the arrow tip to the point, not from arrow bbox\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:28:#   top-left and not from a full line center. After moving k steps, the arrow tip advances by k and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:33:#   D4/2 alone: an arrow attached on the left may still point down, so direction comes from shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:35:#   Combo here: an arrow attached orthogonally to the motion axis points toward a single dot, and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:36:#   the arrow tip rather than any bbox anchor is what aligns to that dot.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:37:#   Example: a right-pointing arrow attached above the container has tip at (r, c). A point sits at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:39:#   cells turn orange; if it has >=5 grey cells, it moves right by 5 and the tip lands on the dot.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:42:# - The arrow vocabulary is still only four shapes, keeping shape parsing low-entropy.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:43:# - To ensure this is visibly different from the source task, the generator only attaches arrows on\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:46:# - The target is always a point with at least one background cell between it and the arrow tip, so\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:51:#   Broken. In the combo, the arrow may be above the box while motion is right.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:55:#   top-left. Broken again: a point has no meaningful shared shape anchor with the arrow. The new\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:56:#   anchor is the arrow tip.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:58:#   between arrow tip and point. The solver still contains a tiny fallback for malformed gap=0\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:59:#   inputs by trying to peel one extra pixel off an otherwise valid arrow component.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:62:# - Each generated assembly reserves the union of five regions: input container, input arrow,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:63:#   destination point, moved container, moved arrow. Packing is done with a 1-cell background moat.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:64:# - Since the point lies on the same ray that the tip follows, both the input and the final state\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:68:# - Across samples, the arrow is visibly not on the side of travel, forcing the solver to read its\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:73:# - Only three foreground object types appear: bordered container, arrow head, point target.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:74:#   The point is sparse, the arrow is compact, and assemblies are separated by background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:78:#   direction, so the only new inference burden is the fused geometry: arrow orientation + tip to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:82:# - Head components are exact 3x3 arrow sprites, or that sprite plus one fused forward point in the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:84:# - Every mover has exactly one nearest forward point on its arrow ray; the generator verifies\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:86:# - Motion direction, fill order, and point alignment are all keyed off the arrow direction, not\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:147:def _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h: int, outer_w: int, attach_side: str, anchor: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:157:def _stamp_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, tl: tuple[int, int], direction: str, color: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:180:def _parse_arrow_component(pts: set[tuple[int, int]]) -> dict | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:184:        tip_local = ARROW_TIPS[direction]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:189:            \"tip\": (int(tl[0] + tip_local[0]), int(tl[1] + tip_local[1])),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:204:        tip_local = ARROW_TIPS[direction]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:205:        tip = (int(rem_tl[0] + tip_local[0]), int(rem_tl[1] + tip_local[1]))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:206:        if p == (int(tip[0] + dr), int(tip[1] + dc)):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:212:                    \"tip\": tip,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:226:    - The source marker is a 3x3 arrow sprite whose orientation gives the movement direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:228:    - Distance is measured from the arrow tip to the target point.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:229:    - Steps = min(tip_to_point_distance, grey_count).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:290:        parsed = _parse_arrow_component(set(comp0[\"pts\"]))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:297:            \"tip\": tuple(parsed[\"tip\"]),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:312:        tip = tuple(head_info[\"tip\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:330:            tr0 = int(p[0] - tip[0])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:331:            tc0 = int(p[1] - tip[1])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:422:        head_r, head_c = _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h, outer_w, attach_side, anchor)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:424:        tip_local = ARROW_TIPS[direction]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:425:        tip_r = int(head_r + tip_local[0])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:426:        tip_c = int(head_c + tip_local[1])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:427:        dest_r = int(tip_r + dr * travel)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:428:        dest_c = int(tip_c + dc * travel)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:531:        _stamp_arrow(grid, (hr, hc), s[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/